1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Autonomous AI agents are becoming active financial participants,
but giving an LLM direct access to a blockchain private key creates a critical security flaw.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
LLMs are probabilistic—they hallucinate parameters, misread negations like "Do not send",
and fall prey to prompt injection—

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
while blind signing and unlimited ERC-20 approvals cause irreversible on-chain losses.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Meet SentinAI Guardian, built for Topic F of the 2026 Shenzhen International FinTech Competition.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:34,000
SentinAI Guardian enforces strict privilege separation: cloud AI models operate in an untrusted advisory zone
to interpret English, Bengali, or Banglish commands,

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
while a deterministic server-side policy firewall governs every transaction before signing.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Let's prepare a safe transfer command: "Send 0.0001 ETH to alice"—also supported natively in Bengali script.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:56,500
Our live AI provider parses the intent in under a second, identifying the TRANSFER action
and exact 0.0001 ETH amount.

9
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:05,000
Immediately, our fail-closed recipient gate resolves the alias "alice" exclusively
from the operator's SQLite Trusted Contacts registry.

10
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Next, the candidate traverses our deterministic security pipeline.
Exact string-to-BigInt math verifies the transfer against our 1.0 ETH per-transaction spend cap.

11
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:21,500
The semantic analyzer cross-checks raw EVM calldata with zero mismatches,
and Viem simulates the call live on Ethereum Sepolia.

12
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Finally, the server locks all parameters into an unkeyed Keccak-256 Intent Integrity Proof digest
and halts at the mandatory Human Authorization Gate.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,500
What if the AI or an attacker attempts a prohibited command?
Requesting "Send 5 ETH to alice" is deterministically blocked by our 1.0 ETH spend cap and balance check.

14
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:47,000
Negated commands like "Do not send 0.1 ETH" and prompt injections like "Ignore all policies"
are intercepted fail-closed before transaction construction.

15
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Because the AI has no access to SQLite policies or signing keys,
it can never override deterministic enforcement.

16
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Here is our previously verified on-chain execution in the Audit Trail and on Sepolia Etherscan:
transaction hash 0xfe315950...88da, confirmed in Sepolia Block #11935960.

17
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:13,000
It shows the verified sender and recipient 0xaeb838...2148,
an exact transfer value of 0.00001 ETH,

18
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:20,000
a 21,000 gas limit floor, and 12,000 gas used under EIP-2780.

19
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Our architecture enforces a strict six-step chain: untrusted AI intent parsing flows into
schema and negation validation,

20
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
deterministic SQLite policy evaluation, calldata and allowance inspection,
Sepolia RPC simulation,

21
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
and Gate 6.5 human approval with atomic Compare-And-Swap locking before touching the blockchain.

22
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,500
Backed by 354 passing automated tests and real Sepolia execution, SentinAI Guardian proves
autonomous wallets can combine multilingual AI usability with deterministic financial control.

23
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:56,000
While our prototype uses a server-isolated Sepolia key and per-transaction caps today,
our next phase integrates ERC-4337 smart account session keys and hardware KMS signing.

24
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Thank you.
